:: rohit_reads_change_d2
Rohit is eating cornflakes from a steel bowl. Dry. No milk. He calls this "a bulk diet technique," not "forgot to buy milk for the fourth day running." He crunches, stops, looks at you, crunches, stops.

<div class="dialogue">"Bhai. Tera face."<span class="gloss-en">Bro. Your face.</span></div>

"Kya hua mere face ko?"<span class="gloss-en">What's wrong with my face?</span>

<div class="dialogue">"Pehle jaisa nahi hai. Kal tak tu aise dikhta tha jaise teri Pulsar ki battery dead hai. Aaj   aaj tera face aisa hai jaise kisi ne jump-start kar diya."<span class="gloss-en">It's not like before. Yesterday you looked like your Pulsar's battery was dead. Today it's like someone jump-started you.</span></div>

You busy yourself with the laptop. "Neend acchi aayi."<span class="gloss-en">Slept well.</span>

<div class="dialogue">"Acchi neend se face glow nahi karta. Glow ke do reasons hain   ladki ya drugs. Aur tu drugs nahi karta   tera idea of rebellion is staying up till 2 AM writing SQL."<span class="gloss-en">Good sleep doesn't make your face glow. Two reasons for glow   a girl, or drugs. And you don't do drugs.</span></div>

He returns to his cornflakes. But his eyes stay on you   the Rohit radar, calibrated over two years of cohabitation.

<<hubreturn>>


:: nikhil_canteen_d2
<<img "nikhil_canteen.png" "The canteen">>
Nikhil slides a plate of aggressively orange pav bhaji across the table. Two toasted pav beside it.

<div class="dialogue">"Bhai. Tu kuch chhupa raha hai. Tera face aisa hai jaise lottery lagi hai aur ticket kho gaya hai."<span class="gloss-en">You're hiding something. Your face is like you won the lottery and lost the ticket.</span></div>

You tear a piece of pav. Your hand shakes. He notices   of course he notices.

He leans forward, glasses catching the light. <div class="dialogue">"Serious question. Agar tujhe Meera se milne ka chance mile   anything, kuch bhi karna pade   tu karega?"<span class="gloss-en">If you got a chance to meet Meera   anything, whatever it took   would you do it?</span></div>

The canteen noise fades. "Anything."

The word doesn't waver. No conditional clauses. Just: anything.

Nikhil shrugs   the precise Nikhil shrug that means he's computed the variables. <div class="dialogue">"Phir jo bhi ho raha hai, kar le. Worst case kya   Colonel tujhe marega? Tu already dead inside hai."<span class="gloss-en">Then do whatever it is. Worst case   the Colonel kills you? You're already dead inside.</span></div>

It lands like a palm strike to the sternum. It hurts and helps in the exact same proportion.

<div class="dialogue">"Also   ICPC practice registration kal last date hai. Two-person teams. Mujhe partner chahiye. Tu in?"<span class="gloss-en">Also   ICPC practice registration closes tomorrow. Two-person teams. I need a partner. You in?</span></div>

<div class="choices">
<<link "\"Haan. I'm in.\"">>
	<<run setup.flag("icpc_agreed")>>
	<<run setup.flag("os_lecture_done")>>
	<<goto "nikhil_canteen_d2_yes">>
<</link>>
<<link "\"Let me think about it.\"">>
	<<goto "nikhil_canteen_d2_no">>
<</link>>
</div>


:: nikhil_canteen_d2_yes
"I'm in."

You mean it in two layers — one for you, one for whoever will be wearing your hands next Saturday.

Nikhil grins. "Good. Because I already put your name down."

<<hubreturn>>


