:: gym_punching_d2
<<img "arjun_gym_punching.png" "Iron Temple Gym">>
<<if setup.has("gym_workout_done")>>\
The bag is where you left it. You hit it a few more times. It absorbs everything, with the dumb patience of an object built to be struck.
<<hubreturn>>
<<else>>\
<div class="dialogue">"Arjun! Aa gaya finally. Rohit bol raha tha tu gym chhodne wala hai."<span class="gloss-en">You came, finally. Rohit said you were about to quit.</span></div>

Vikram wraps your hands   boxing tape, figure-eight over the knuckles. Your hands become padded weapons.

You hit the bag. First punch tentative. Second harder. By the fifth you're not hitting a bag   you're hitting six months of silence, the Colonel's surveillance grid, every night spent staring at a phone waiting for a message that never came.

Your arms burn. Sweat runs down your chest, your back. The machine performing at capacity. You're aware of your body in a way you usually aren't   the weight of your fists, the torque in your hips, the small constant adjustments a male body makes with gravity.

Tomorrow morning someone else will be wearing this. A girl named Kavya will look down and see these hands.

You hit the bag one more time. Hard. Catch it with both hands. Press your forehead to the leather. Breathe.

<div class="dialogue">"Bahut gussa hai," Vikram observes from the bench. "Ya bahut pyaar. Same energy   different direction."<span class="gloss-en">That's a lot of anger. Or a lot of love. Same energy   different direction.</span></div>

<<run setup.flag("gym_workout_done")>>
<<run setup.stat("energy", 8, "arjun")>>
<<hubreturn>>
<</if>>


:: gym_weights_d2
<div class="intimate"><span class="tag">Intimate · body awareness</span>

You load the bar. Squats. Going down, the weight of your own body plus iron; coming up, the drive through the heels. And the small thing you never think about   your balls shifting in your underwear at the bottom of the rep, the constant minor negotiation of having testicles subject to momentum.

You don't think about it. It's nothing. Tomorrow you won't have it to not-think-about.
</div>

<<run setup.stat("energy", 4, "arjun")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: gym_mirror_d2
The gym mirror is cloudy but honest. Lean swimmer's frame. Flat chest, ribs when you raise your arms. The body you've had for twenty-one years and never once had to think about wearing.

<<hubreturn>>


:: gym_vikram_d2
Vikram spots a first-year on the bench press, counting reps in a voice that sounds like it does push-ups. Between sets he tells you Rohit benches 90 now, that the flickering tube light has been "next week's job" for a year, and that whatever's eating you, "gym mein chhod ke jaana. Bahar mat le jaana."<span class="gloss-en">leave it at the gym. Don't take it outside.</span>

<<hubreturn>>


:: temple_priest_d2
<<img "temple_priest.png" "Pataleshwar, evening">>
Pataleshwar. "Lord of the Underworld"   Shiva in his cave-dwelling form. Carved from one basalt rock, sitting in modern Pune like a memory the city grew around without absorbing.

The courtyard is golden at 5 PM   the light that makes old stone look alive. An old priest tends an oil lamp at the cave mouth. White dhoti, rudraksha, ash in three lines on his forehead.

<div class="dialogue">"Pahli baar aaye ho?"<span class="gloss-en">First time here?</span> He doesn't look up.</div>

"Haan. Bas dekh raha tha."<span class="gloss-en">Yes. Just looking.</span>

<div class="dialogue">"Dekhne mein kya hai   pathar hai, mandir hai, Mahadev hain. But log aate hain. Aate hain seeking change. Job, shaadi, baccha, bimari se chutkara. Sab aate hain."<span class="gloss-en">What's there to see   it's stone, a temple, Shiva. But people come. They come seeking change. A job, marriage, a child, relief from illness. They all come.</span></div>

You walk the courtyard. Map it: the cave (locked, opens 6 AM), the Nandi facing it, roughly fifteen by twenty metres of stone floor, two benches on the far wall, neem trees for cover on the east, the road at a distance on the west.

At 5 AM this place will be empty. No priest, no devotees, no witnesses.

"Kal subah 5 baje aaunga," you tell him. "Ek dost ke saath."<span class="gloss-en">I'll come tomorrow at 5 AM. With a friend.</span>

<div class="dialogue">"5 baje Mahadev bhi sote hain. But courtyard khula hai. Jo chahiye karo."<span class="gloss-en">At 5, even Shiva sleeps. But the courtyard is open. Do what you need to.</span></div>

<<run setup.flag("visited_pataleshwar")>>
<<run setup.flag("temple_scouted")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: temple_courtyard_d2
You pace the courtyard slowly. Fifteen by twenty metres of stone, worn smooth by fifteen centuries of feet. Two benches against the far wall. Neem trees on the east side, thick enough to stand behind. The road on the west, close enough to hear an auto but far enough that nobody in one would see two people sitting cross-legged on the ground at dawn.

You'll know where to sit tomorrow. That's worth something.

<<run setup.flag("visited_pataleshwar")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: temple_nandi_d2
The Nandi is carved from the same basalt as everything else   a bull the size of a small car, facing the cave, waiting for a god to come out. It has been waiting fifteen hundred years. You feel, absurdly, that you have something in common with it.

<<run setup.flag("visited_pataleshwar")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: temple_prayer_d2
You sit on the cold stone in front of the dark cave mouth and don't pray, exactly. You just sit. Tomorrow you'll be one of the people the priest talked about   the ones who come here seeking change. Tonight you're just a boy on a rock, rehearsing hope.

<<run setup.flag("visited_pataleshwar")>>
<<hubreturn>>


