<<else>>\
<<img "jm_road.png" "JM Road">>
The Crossword on JM Road has a small, curated CS section. The employee   twenty-three, kurta and jeans, lit-postgrad-working-retail energy   sees you in the algorithms rack.

<div class="dialogue">"Looking for something specific?"</div>

"Data structures. Not Cormen   I have Cormen. Something different."

<div class="dialogue">"Skiena. Algorithm Design Manual. Practical, opinionated, less theoretical."</div>

You flip through it. //Kavya would like this//   the thought arrives before you can stop it, because some part of you has already accepted that tomorrow someone else carries your books.

"₹650. Student discount, ₹550."

You buy it. It's heavy in your bag   a physical weight of knowledge that might pass through hands you haven't met.

<<run setup.flag("skiena_bought")>>
<<run setup.addMoney(-550, "arjun")>>
<<hubreturn>>
<</if>>


:: jm_food_d2
You get bhel from a lit cart   puffed rice, onion, tamarind, a squeeze of lime, sev on top. Eat it standing on the pavement watching Pune's cultural heart do its evening thing: bookshop bags, couples, an auto arguing with a scooter. For ten minutes you are just a person eating bhel, not a person about to leave his body.

<<run setup.stat("energy", 3, "arjun")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: jm_people_watch_d2
You sit on a roadside bench. An auto driver   fifties, handlebar mustache, khaki   parks nearby and orders chai from a cart. He clocks your VIT bag.

<div class="dialogue">"Mera ladka bhi engineer banna chahta tha. 87% aaya boards mein. Seat ka donation das lakh maanga. Ab mere saath auto chalata hai."<span class="gloss-en">My son wanted to be an engineer too. Got 87% in the boards. The seat donation was ten lakh. Now he drives an auto with me.</span></div>

His name is Sanjay. His son drives nights so Sanjay can drive days. Together they cover rent, dal-chawal, and the younger daughter's school fees.

<div class="dialogue">"Is desh mein degree se zyada zaroorat connections ki hai. Degree khali dabba hai. Andar kuch ho tab kaam aaye."<span class="gloss-en">In this country you need connections more than a degree. A degree's an empty box. It only helps if there's something inside.</span></div>

He pays exact change, tips an imaginary hat, and drives into the traffic. You sit with your privilege: VIT fees are ₹1.8 lakh a year. Papa's LIC salary covers it with effort. Sanjay's earnings wouldn't cover a semester's lab charges. Tomorrow you might be in someone else's body   but you have a body in a college. You have a seat.

<<hubreturn>>
